  <middle>
    <section anchor="introduction" numbered="true" removeInRFC="false" toc="include" pn="section-1">
      <name slugifiedName="name-introduction">Introduction</name>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-1-1">This document specifies the 'tn3270' Uniform Resource Identifier
(URI) scheme, which is used to designate the access to the resources
available via Telnet 3270 mode (TN3270) and Telnet 3270 Enhanced mode
(TN3270E).</t>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-1-2">Telnet 3270 mode (TN3270) is a name used to denote the special mode
of Telnet session <xref target="RFC0854" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC0854"/>.  If it is used, the 3270 data stream
<xref target="GA23-0059" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="GA23-0059"/> is used when transmitting data during the Telnet session,
whereas the Telnet 3270 Regime option <xref target="RFC1041" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC1041"/> is used to enable and
negotiate its use.  (See <xref target="RFC1576" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC1576"/> for some other background
information on TN3270.)  Telnet 3270 Enhanced mode (TN3270E) has a
similar principle; see RFC 2355 <xref target="RFC2355" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC2355"/> for its specification.</t>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-1-3">The 'tn3270' URI scheme was firstly mentioned in RFC 1738 <xref target="RFC1738" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC1738"/>
as used in URIs that designate the access to &quot;Interactive 3270
emulation sessions&quot;.  Following the creation of the URI schemes
registry per RFC 4395 <xref target="RFC4395" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC4395"/>, this scheme was added to the
&quot;Provisional URI Schemes&quot; sub-registry in the &quot;Uniform Resource
Identifier (URI) Schemes&quot; IANA registry <xref target="URIREG" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="URIREG"/>.  However, RFC 1738
<xref target="RFC1738" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC1738"/> does not give any definition of syntax or semantics of the
'tn3270' URIs and does not have any guidelines for registration of
this scheme.</t>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-1-4">Since there is no acceptable specification of the 'tn3270' URI, there
is a risk that somebody might try to implement it with some new,
possibly undocumented, syntax, just by looking at the IANA registry.
In order to minimize such risk, this document gives a precise
definition of syntax, semantics, use of this URI, and it registers
the corresponding scheme.  It also updates RFC 2355 <xref target="RFC2355" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC2355"/>, RFC
1738 <xref target="RFC1738" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC1738"/>, and RFC 1041 <xref target="RFC1041" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC1041"/>.</t>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-1-5">The generic syntax of URIs is described in RFC 3986 <xref target="RFC3986" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC3986"/>.
Registration procedures for new URI schemes are defined in RFC 4395
<xref target="RFC4395" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC4395"/>.</t>
      <section anchor="terminology" numbered="true" removeInRFC="false" toc="include" pn="section-1.1">
        <name slugifiedName="name-terminology">Terminology</name>
        <t indent="0" pn="section-1.1-1">The key words &quot;<bcp14>MUST</bcp14>&quot;, &quot;<bcp14>MUST NOT</bcp14>&quot;, &quot;<bcp14>REQUIRED</bcp14>&quot;, &quot;<bcp14>SHALL</bcp14>&quot;, &quot;<bcp14>SHALL NOT</bcp14>&quot;,
&quot;<bcp14>SHOULD</bcp14>&quot;, &quot;<bcp14>SHOULD NOT</bcp14>&quot;, &quot;<bcp14>RECOMMENDED</bcp14>&quot;, &quot;<bcp14>MAY</bcp14>&quot;, and &quot;<bcp14>OPTIONAL</bcp14>&quot; in this
document are to be interpreted as described in RFC 2119 <xref target="RFC2119" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC2119"/>.</t>
      </section>
    </section>
    <section anchor="uri-scheme-definition" numbered="true" removeInRFC="false" toc="include" pn="section-2">
      <name slugifiedName="name-uri-scheme-definition">URI Scheme Definition</name>
      <section anchor="uri-scheme-syntax" numbered="true" removeInRFC="false" toc="include" pn="section-2.1">
        <name slugifiedName="name-uri-scheme-syntax">URI Scheme Syntax</name>
        <t indent="0" pn="section-2.1-1">The 'tn3270' URI takes the following form (the syntax below is non-normative):</t>
        <artwork name="" type="" align="left" alt="" pn="section-2.1-2"><![CDATA[
   tn3270://<userinfo>@<host>:<port>/
]]></artwork>
        <t indent="0" pn="section-2.1-3">where the &lt;userinfo&gt; part with the &quot;@&quot; (at) sign character, as well
as the &lt;port&gt; part with the preceding &quot;:&quot; (colon) character, is
<bcp14>OPTIONAL</bcp14>.</t>
        <t indent="0" pn="section-2.1-4">The normative syntax of the 'tn3270' URI is defined in &lt;tn3270-uri&gt;
ABNF <xref target="RFC5234" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC5234"/> rule:</t>
        <sourcecode type="abnf" markers="false" pn="section-2.1-5"><![CDATA[
  tn3270-uri       = "tn3270:" "//" authority ["/"]
]]></sourcecode>
        <t indent="0" pn="section-2.1-6">where the &lt;authority&gt; rule is specified in RFC 3986 <xref target="RFC3986" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC3986"/>.</t>
        <t indent="0" pn="section-2.1-7">The semantics of each part of the 'tn3270' URI are defined below, in
<xref target="uri-scheme-semantics" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="Section 2.2"/>.</t>
      </section>
      <section anchor="uri-scheme-semantics" numbered="true" removeInRFC="false" toc="include" pn="section-2.2">
        <name slugifiedName="name-uri-scheme-semantics">URI Scheme Semantics</name>
        <t indent="0" pn="section-2.2-1">The &lt;host&gt; part of the 'tn3270' URI, which is <bcp14>REQUIRED</bcp14>, denotes the
host to which the TN3270 or TN3270E connection is to be established.
The &lt;userinfo&gt; part is considered to define the information for use
in the Telnet Authentication option <xref target="RFC2941" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC2941"/>, which might be used
during the TN3270 or TN3270E session.  The &lt;port&gt; part, if present,
denotes the port on which the TCP connection to the &lt;host&gt; is to be
established.  If it is absent, the default port <bcp14>SHALL</bcp14> be 23, as
registered in <xref target="PORTREG" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="PORTREG"/>.</t>
      </section>
    </section>
    <section anchor="security-considerations" numbered="true" removeInRFC="false" toc="include" pn="section-3">
      <name slugifiedName="name-security-considerations">Security Considerations</name>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-3-1">Generic security considerations for the usage of URIs are discussed
in <xref target="RFC3986" section="7" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedLink="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3986#section-7" derivedContent="RFC3986"/>.</t>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-3-2">Since 'tn3270' URIs provide access to services that are available via
TN3270 and TN3270E, which do not add any new security issues to the
Telnet session, as they are a modified form of it, there are no other
security considerations for 'tn3270' URIs that are not discussed in
RFC 4248 <xref target="RFC4248" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC4248"/>, the 'telnet' URI scheme specification.</t>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-3-3">The Telnet protocol, as well as TN3270 and TN3270E, is inherently
insecure.  Those needing remote login access and related services are
encouraged to use a more secure technology, such as Secure Shell
<xref target="RFC4251" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC4251"/>.</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="iana-considerations" numbered="true" removeInRFC="false" toc="include" pn="section-4">
      <name slugifiedName="name-iana-considerations">IANA Considerations</name>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-4-1">IANA updated the registration of the 'tn3270' URI scheme using the
following registration template (see <xref target="RFC4395" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC4395"/>):</t>
      <dl newline="false" spacing="normal" indent="3" pn="section-4-2">
        <dt pn="section-4-2.1">URI scheme name:</dt>
        <dd pn="section-4-2.2">tn3270</dd>
        <dt pn="section-4-2.3">Status:</dt>
        <dd pn="section-4-2.4">Permanent</dd>
        <dt pn="section-4-2.5">URI scheme syntax:</dt>
        <dd pn="section-4-2.6">see Section 2.1 of RFC 6270</dd>
        <dt pn="section-4-2.7">URI scheme semantics:</dt>
        <dd pn="section-4-2.8">see Section 2.2 of RFC 6270</dd>
        <dt pn="section-4-2.9">URI scheme encoding considerations:</dt>
        <dd pn="section-4-2.10">there are no other encoding
considerations for 'tn3270' URIs that are not described in RFC
3986 <xref target="RFC3986" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC3986"/></dd>
        <dt pn="section-4-2.11">Protocols that use the scheme:</dt>
        <dd pn="section-4-2.12">Telnet 3270 mode (TN3270) <xref target="RFC1041" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC1041"/>
and Telnet 3270 Enhanced Mode (TN3270E) <xref target="RFC2355" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC2355"/></dd>
        <dt pn="section-4-2.13">Security considerations:</dt>
        <dd pn="section-4-2.14">see Section 3 of RFC 6270</dd>
        <dt pn="section-4-2.15">Contact:</dt>
        <dd pn="section-4-2.16">IESG &lt;iesg@ietf.org&gt;</dd>
        <dt pn="section-4-2.17">Author/Change controller:</dt>
        <dd pn="section-4-2.18">IETF &lt;ietf@ietf.org&gt;</dd>
        <dt pn="section-4-2.19">References:</dt>
        <dd pn="section-4-2.20">see Section 5 of RFC 6270</dd>
      </dl>
    </section>
